      "fp.voice-logging.recTitle": "It becomes part of the day, immediately.",
      "fp.voice-logging.recBody": "A spoken entry is not a second-class one. The moment you save it, it is the same object as a photographed meal, and everything downstream treats it that way.",
      "fp.voice-logging.faqTitle": "Questions about voice logging",
      "fp.voice-logging.ctaTitle": "Log your next meal without typing a word.",
      "fp.voice-logging.ctaBody": "Fotocal is free to start, with a 5-day free trial on the paid plans.",
      "fp.voice-logging.relatedTitle": "Keep reading",
      "fp.voice-logging.relatedSub": "Short, practical reads that go deeper on tracking without the busywork.",
      "fp.voice-logging.navLabel": "Voice logging",
      "fp.voice-logging.bandAlt": "A woman mid-cook in a sunlit kitchen, talking to the phone in her hand while vegetables wait on the board",
      "fp.voice-logging.bandPill": "In practice",
      "fp.voice-logging.bandTitle": "Hands full, meal logged",
      "fp.voice-logging.bandBody": "Halfway through cooking is exactly when you cannot type. Say what went into the pan and it is recorded — no rinsing your hands first, no picking the phone up twice.",
      "fp.voice-logging.s1t": "Tap the microphone",
      "fp.voice-logging.s1b": "It sits on the logging screen alongside photo and barcode. Nothing to set up, no wake word, no separate account to connect.",
      "fp.voice-logging.s2t": "Talk like a person",
      "fp.voice-logging.s2b": "“Two scrambled eggs, a slice of rye toast and a flat white.” No app grammar, no unit conversions, no picking items off a list. Ordinary sentences are the input format.",
      "fp.voice-logging.s3t": "It works out the language",
      "fp.voice-logging.s3b": "You never set one first. Speak Spanish today and English tomorrow, or switch mid-sentence because a dish only has a name in one of them — Fotocal detects what it heard and transcribes in that language.",
      "fp.voice-logging.s4t": "Check it, then send it to a meal",
      "fp.voice-logging.s4b": "You get the transcript and every item it found with its quantity. Fix anything that looks wrong, choose Breakfast, Lunch, Dinner or Snack, and save.",
      "fp.voice-logging.c1t": "The transcript",
      "fp.voice-logging.c1b": "Exactly what it heard, in the language you spoke, so it is always obvious why it logged what it logged.",
      "fp.voice-logging.c2t": "Every food, separated",
      "fp.voice-logging.c2b": "One line per item rather than one lump. “Chicken salad with dressing” comes back as chicken, salad and dressing, each with its own numbers.",
      "fp.voice-logging.c3t": "Real quantities",
      "fp.voice-logging.c3b": "“A handful of almonds”, “two slices”, “a large coffee” — spoken amounts are read as amounts and converted into grams or millilitres.",
      "fp.voice-logging.c4t": "Calories per item and in total",
      "fp.voice-logging.c4b": "Each food carries its own figure, so you can see which part of the meal actually cost you rather than only the final number.",
      "fp.voice-logging.c5t": "The full macro split",
      "fp.voice-logging.c5b": "Protein, carbs and fat for the entry, added into the day's rings the moment you save it.",
      "fp.voice-logging.c6t": "Any meal, any time",
      "fp.voice-logging.c6b": "Breakfast, Lunch, Dinner or Snack — including logging last night's dinner over this morning's coffee.",
      "fp.voice-logging.r1": "Your calories-left figure recalculates on the spot, along with the protein, carb and fat rings.",
      "fp.voice-logging.r2": "Coach Kal can see it. Ask “what should I have for dinner?” and the answer accounts for what you just said you ate.",
      "fp.voice-logging.r3": "It feeds the Recommendations tab, which suggests changes based on where your day is actually short rather than on a generic template.",
      "fp.voice-logging.r4": "It counts toward your Weekly Report, so a week logged by voice is as complete as a week logged by camera.",
      "fp.voice-logging.q1": "Which languages does it understand?",
      "fp.voice-logging.a1": "It detects the language automatically instead of asking you to choose one, so you can speak whichever language the food actually has a name in. That matters more than it sounds — plenty of dishes have no good translation, and describing them in their own language gives a better result than describing them in English.",
      "fp.voice-logging.q2": "Do I have to phrase it a particular way?",
      "fp.voice-logging.a2": "No. Full sentences, half sentences and lists all work. You do not need to say units, though saying them helps: “200 grams of rice” will always beat “some rice”.",
      "fp.voice-logging.q3": "What if it mishears something?",
      "fp.voice-logging.a3": "Nothing is saved until you tap save. Until then every detected item can be edited, re-weighed or removed, and you can add anything it missed by hand.",
      "fp.voice-logging.q4": "Does it need an internet connection?",
      "fp.voice-logging.a4": "Yes. Both the transcription and the nutrition estimate happen on our side, so voice logging needs a connection to work.",
